ation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+ s\\mathbf{b}+ t \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,5 \\rangle,$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-3,2,5⟩+s\\,⟨17,3,15⟩+t\\,⟨5,0,-8⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041396/example-4940.json" "file_sha256" "8ad9695945ef2a9f483083123c9994647dc8b1b992525b222e745dd1bd62d00c"} {"question" "e-5884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a normal vector to the plane \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}= \\langle -6,3, 2 \\rangle + t \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle + s \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle,\\qquad t,\\,s \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041396/example-5884.json" "file_sha256" "3d9c77493483b99c6c5a55db0f0ee6b3168cd000e7c171b8a0eb509aa488ee9d"} {"question" "e-5884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{w} = \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i-12j-20k \\\\  & =⟨1,-12,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 1, -12, -20 \\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041396/example-5884.json" "file_sha256" "3d9c77493483b99c6c5a55db0f0ee6b3168cd000e7c171b8a0eb509aa488ee9d"} {"question" "e-4941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $C(2,50,-19)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}= \\langle 2,\\sqrt{6},17 \\rangle + s \\langle -1,7,2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{r}= \\langle 20,-44, 27 \\rangle + t \\langle 2\\sqrt{3},9,-8 \\rangle$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041396/example-4941.json" "file_sha256" "f45f164cd323edb01de11e0020b8711625a174d76a373829d04981efba8bd8ad"} {"question" "e-4941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The direction vectors of the lines are $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle  -1,7,2  \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 2\\sqrt{3},9,-8 \\rangle$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $C(2,50,-19)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 2,50,-19 \\rangle.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =c+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨2,50,-19⟩+s\\,⟨-1,7,2⟩+t\\,⟨2\\sqrt{3},9,-8⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041396/example-4941.json" "file_sha256" "f45f164cd323edb01de11e0020b8711625a174d76a373829d04981efba8bd8ad"} {"question" "e-4942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a parametric equation of t